# Weather and light that read as weather, played through the real keys:
# a lamp that lays nothing on a bright land and a warm pool on a dark one, snow
# drawn pale against pale, a whiteout that takes the far field, dry lightning
# with its afterglow, and a wet night where the lights carry into the air.
#   tools/tour.sh tours/sky-polish.tour --seed=1
coast calm

# Noon on the snowfield, the brightest ground in the game. Press the real lamp
# key: the land must not change. (It used to lay a saturated pink disc here.)
place snowfield
hour 12
weather clear:0
wait 1.2
shot 01-snow-noon-drift-a
wait 0.25
shot 02-snow-noon-drift-b
tap lamp
wait 0.25
shot 03-snow-noon-lamp-lit
# Two frames of a running world are never identical: cloud shade walks, tufts
# sway, stipple crawls. So the first pair measures that drift over a quarter of
# a second with nothing done at all, and the second pair must come in under the
# same number with the lamp lit between them — the lamp adds no more to the page
# than standing still does. The crop is centred on the player, over the whole
# oval the disc used to cover (the playtest measured it about 470x330 shot
# pixels across, around the hand).
same 01-snow-noon-drift-a 02-snow-noon-drift-b 0.010 400,195,480,330
same 02-snow-noon-drift-b 03-snow-noon-lamp-lit 0.010 400,195,480,330
tap lamp
wait 0.4

# The same ground at night: now the lamp is the light, and the pool it lays is
# its own warm, in flat steps, over cold snow.
hour 23
wait 0.8
shot 04-snow-night-lamp-out
tap lamp
wait 0.6
shot 05-snow-night-lamp-lit
tap lamp
wait 0.4

# Snow falling on lying snow: paper flecks held by a shade, never dark specks.
hour 13
weather snow:1
wait 1.4
shot 06-snowfield-snow-pale-flecks
# A whiteout: the far field goes in blowing white, the near field still reads.
weather whiteout:1
wait 1.4
shot 07-snowfield-whiteout

# The bonelands at dusk: dry lightning with no rain in it. Wait for a real
# strike, then catch the light rolling on through the cloud.
place bonelands
hour 20.2
weather dry_storm:1
wait 1.0
shot 08-bonelands-dry-storm
await strike 60
wait 0.35
shot 09-bonelands-afterglow-rolls
# The drawn bolt lives four frames: too quick to be sure of catching by waiting
# for one, and a tour that only sometimes has a bolt in its picture proves
# nothing. So the still that must show one asks the sky to hold it — the same
# held moment a shot gets (10_sky HELD_STRIKE), the cloud at its brightest with
# the machines' power back up between two of its dips.
weather dry_storm:1:bolt
wait 0.5
shot 10-bonelands-bolt-held
# Hard white glare on the same stone at noon.
hour 12.5
weather glare:1
wait 1.2
shot 11-bonelands-glare

# The burning's furnace haze, lying low and warm at dusk.
place burning
hour 19.4
weather haze:1
wait 1.4
shot 12-burning-furnace-haze

# A wet night in the first village: every pool the colour of the light that
# made it, the stolen neon spilling into the rain, and the lantern walked
# through it. Then the same village at noon, where nothing spills at all.
village 0
hour 22.5
weather rain:0.8
wait 1.6
shot 13-village-wet-night
tap lamp
wait 0.6
shot 14-village-wet-lantern
walk 1,0 1.2
shot 15-walked-with-the-lantern
tap lamp
hour 12
wait 1.2
shot 16-village-wet-noon

# The village square at 23:00, close in: the frame a pool must not flatten. The
# fire's logs against its flames, the bench, the cobbles and the player's own
# coat, trousers and boots all still read inside the lantern's own light.
hour 23
weather clear:0
zoom 9
wait 1.2
shot 17-village-night-pool-out
tap lamp
wait 0.8
shot 18-village-night-pool-lit
tap lamp
weather rules
